=== night_5 ===

= front_entrance

You don't go to the staff entrance.

You walk past it. Past the sign that reads ALL STAFF MUST CHECK IN. Past the corridor that leads to the costume room, the break room, the locker where your dead miner jumpsuit hangs on a hook. Past the fluorescent lights and the laminated warnings and the clipboard and the smile.

You walk to the front of the building. To the queue.

Two hundred people in line. Maybe more. Friday night. The biggest crowd of the week. They're loud. Excited. Nervous. A couple near the front is taking a selfie with the mine entrance behind them. A group of teenagers dares each other to go first. A father holds his daughter's hand.

Nobody looks at you twice. You're not in costume. No greasepaint, no mask, no black base layer. Just clothes. Just a person in a line. The ticket scanners beep. Tickets are taken and wristbands go on. The crowd surges forward.

{erasure_level >= 7: Nobody looks at you at all. You move through the line like a gap in the crowd. People step aside without noticing they've stepped aside. The ticket scanner beeps but the attendant's eyes slide past you. You're not invisible. You're just not quite enough to register.}

{erasure_level < 7: A few people glance at you. A woman smiles. A kid stares. Normal crowd behavior. You smile back. You are a person in a line. For the first time in five days, you are not performing. You are not an actor, not an agent, not a dead miner behind a window. You're a guest. The mine doesn't care about the difference.}

The entrance arch swallows the crowd. Red light. Fog. The speakers boom a recorded warning about heart conditions and seizures and the right to leave at any time by saying the safe word.

Mercy.

You walk in.

-> the_collapse_return

= the_collapse_return

Zone 2. The Collapse.

Your first zone. Night 1, Position B, boo hole three. The corridor is narrower than you remember. You've been in tighter spaces since. The boo hole. The rubble gap. The wall in Zone 8. After those, everything should feel spacious. It doesn't. The fog pools at your ankles. The amber light washes everything in the color of old blood.

The group ahead of you is bunched together. Four people. They shuffle through the narrow corridor, hands on each other's shoulders. The timber supports lean at bad angles. The foam rubble is piled against the walls. The ceiling presses down.

You know what's coming. You know exactly what's coming. Boo hole three is ten feet ahead, on the right. The spring-loaded window. The pneumatic latch. The actor behind it, watching through the slit, waiting for the first person to cross the glow tape mark.

You brace yourself.

The window slams open. The crack echoes off the stone walls. A scream, short, sharp, from the chest. The group recoils. Someone shrieks. Someone laughs. The window bangs shut. The latch clicks.

You flinched. You knew it was coming, you knew the timing, the angle, the exact sound the pneumatic hinge makes when it fires, and you flinched anyway. Your shoulders jumped. Your breath caught.

Six hours on the other side of that window and the startle still lands.

The group pushes forward. You follow. Past boo hole three. Past the position where Bex used to do the stalker walk, herding guests toward the window with silent precision. Someone else is there now. Or no one. The corridor feels understaffed.

At the far end of The Collapse, where the corridor curves toward the transition to Zone 3, the group rounds the bend and disappears.

You stop.

The Figure is standing at the end of the corridor.

-> figure_leads

= figure_leads

The burlap mask. The faded black base layers. The posture you've been seeing since Night 1: weight slightly forward, one hand at the side, the other half-raised. Motionless. Facing you.

{mara_clues >= 4: Mara. You don't guess anymore. You don't qualify it with "might be" or "what's left of." Mara Castillo is standing at the end of The Collapse corridor, waiting for you the way she's been waiting since the night you arrived.}

{mara_clues >= 2 && mara_clues < 4: The Figure. The same one. Every night, closer. Every night, more deliberate. Whatever it is, it's been leading you here. To this corridor, on this night, with nothing between you and it but fog.}

{mara_clues < 2: The Figure. You still don't know what it is. But you know it's been consistent. It appears. It watches. It points down. Tonight it's not pointing.}

The Figure turns. Slowly. The burlap mask rotates away from you. The jumpsuit shifts with a stiffness that isn't fabric. It's facing the wall now. The maintenance door.

You know this door. Not this one. The type. The Shaft: the maintenance grate at the back of the staging alcove, corroded hinge, warm air that moved in a rhythm ventilation doesn't have. {visited_restricted_passage: The passage that went deeper than the show.} But this isn't that grate. This is a door in the wall of The Collapse that wasn't here five nights ago, or wasn't visible, or was always here and the mine just hadn't shown it to you yet.

The Figure pushes the door open. No handle. No latch. The door moves because the Figure moves it. The passage behind it exhales warm air into the corridor. Copper. Ozone. Sulfur. Deep stone.

The Figure walks through.

It doesn't vanish. It doesn't flicker. It walks, one step at a time, into the passage and down.

Behind you, from the upper zones, screaming. Guests. The show is running. Hundreds of people in the corridors above, feeding the machine. The screams echo down through the stone, layered and distorted, and for a moment they don't sound like performance. They sound real. They sound like forty-eight men in a mine shaft that just opened underneath them.

* [Follow.]
    -> descent_night_5

= descent_night_5

You step through the door.

The passage is the same one you've been walking for five nights, through different entrances, at different depths. The maintenance tunnel. The service corridor. The deep rooms. All of them were routes to the same destination. The mine has been showing you the way in pieces, a little deeper each night, and tonight it opens the whole path at once.

The Figure is ahead of you. Twenty feet. Walking. The burlap mask doesn't turn. The footsteps are silent on the stone. You follow.

The screaming from above continues. It filters through the rock, muted but present. Every few seconds a burst of sound reaches you: a shriek, a laugh, the bang of a prop mechanism firing. The show running at full power. The biggest crowd of the week pouring fear into the machine.

The passage descends. The set dressing is gone. No plywood, no paint, no speakers. Raw stone. The utility lights thin out. Yellow pools, further and further apart, with longer stretches of dark between them.

The Figure walks. You follow.

The screaming from above changes as you descend. It doesn't get quieter. It gets clearer. The distortion strips away and underneath it you hear the actual sound: not the theatrical scream of someone being startled by a prop, but something rawer. A frequency that doesn't come from surprise. It comes from the body, from the autonomic system, from the part of the brain that knows before the conscious mind does.

Real fear. The guests are really afraid. Not startled. Not thrilled. Afraid.

The portal is breathing faster tonight. You feel it in the air pressure: the exhale pushes past you, hot and heavy, copper and sulfur, then the contraction pulls you forward. Exhale. Inhale. Exhale. The rhythm is shorter than last night. The four-second exhale has compressed to three. The two-second pause is a second and a half. Everything is accelerating.

{has_ritual_knowledge: A second and a half. The window is closing. Last night you counted two seconds between breaths. Tonight it's less. Tomorrow there might not be a pause at all.}

The utility lights end.

The last yellow pool falls behind you. Ahead, the only light is amber. Pulsing. Coming from below. The glow fills the tunnel in waves, brighter dimmer brighter dimmer, and the Figure walks through it without casting a shadow.

{mara_clues >= 3: "She went down. Past the last light. She's still there."}

{mara_clues >= 3: The actor's words from last night. Not show dialogue. Something the mine pushed through them. A message, or a fact, or both. Past the last light. You're past the last light now. The amber is all there is.}

The Figure stops.

You're at the top of the spiral steps. The same steps from last night. Rough-cut stone, old, descending in a tight helix. The amber light pulses from below. The hum is so strong here that your bones resonate with it. Your jaw vibrates. The fillings in your teeth sing.

The Figure turns. Faces you. The burlap mask, featureless, eyeless. It stands at the top of the spiral for a long moment. Then it steps down. One step. Another. Descending.

You follow.

The spiral steps are worn smooth in the center. The same grooves from last night. But tonight the stone is warm under your feet, pulsing with the portal's rhythm, and the amber light is brighter. Much brighter. The glow doesn't just fill the stairwell. It saturates it. The air itself is luminous. Your hands, when you touch the wall for balance, glow at the edges.

{erasure_level >= 7: Your hands glow. Or the light passes through them. You can't tell the difference anymore. The edges of your fingers are soft. Translucent. You are running out of time and substance both.}

The screaming from above is distant now. A murmur. The portal's breathing is the only sound. Exhale. Inhale. Exhale. The walls pulse. The steps pulse. Everything here is alive with the same rhythm.

The Figure reaches the bottom. Steps off the last stair into the cavern. The portal chamber.

You stand on the final step.

[Look.]

-> portal_chamber

= portal_chamber

The cavern is the same as last night. Natural stone, curved walls, smooth as if shaped by water or pressure or something that wanted the space to be exactly this shape. The amber light fills it, sourceless. The air is hot. The hum is a pressure on your chest, on your skull, in the fluid behind your eyes.

Dale is not here. The steps where he stood last night are empty. Whatever kept him between you and the portal last night, it's not keeping him tonight. He's above, running the show, managing the machine, feeding the system. Or he's dissolved into the system itself, the manager absorbed by the thing he managed. It doesn't matter. The path is clear.

The portal.

You've felt it for five days. The hum. The heat. The pull. Now you see it.

It's not a hole. It's not a door. It's a place in the air where the cavern stops being a cavern. The stone floor ends in a circle, maybe fifteen feet across, and inside the circle there is no floor. No rock. No anything. Just depth. A shaft that goes down into something that isn't earth. The amber light rises from it the way heat rises from asphalt, shimmering, distorting the air above it, and if you look straight down you see nothing. Not darkness. Nothing. An absence so complete it has texture.

The carvings ring the edge. Concentric arcs, angular intersections. The containment pattern, or what's left of it. Some of the lines are sharp and deep. Some are almost smooth, worn away by decades. Some have been freshly recut. {bex_relationship >= 5:  Bex's work.} And some have been scored through with new marks, deliberate vandalism, the feeding and opening glyphs that HorrorCo added to widen the portal inch by inch over twenty-seven years.

The Figure stands at the far edge of the circle. Facing you across the portal through the burlap mask. Motionless.

The portal exhales. The force hits you. Heat, pressure, a wall of sulfur so thick you gag on it. Your throat closes. Your eyes flood. A wave of something heavier than air pushes you back a step. Your skin burns. The amber light flares.

Then the contraction. A second and a half of stillness. The portal draws inward. The pressure drops. The air cools, briefly, a pocket of silence between breaths.

Then the exhale again.

You stand at the edge. The Figure stands opposite. Between you, the thing that has been eating this town for fifty years.

// ========================
// BRANCHING LOGIC
// ========================

// Check order:
// 1. Both paths available (choice point)
// 2. Other Side only
// 3. Buried only
// 4. Default (neither)

{has_ritual_knowledge && has_mara_pages && (has_symbol_knowledge || has_portal_mechanics): -> portal_choice}
{not has_ritual_knowledge && has_mara_pages && (has_symbol_knowledge || has_portal_mechanics) && erasure_level < 8: -> other_side_ending}
{has_ritual_knowledge: -> buried_ending}
-> night_5_descent_default

= portal_choice

// Both endings available. Player has ritual knowledge AND Mara's full analysis AND
// portal understanding AND enough self left to act.

You know the pattern. The containment circuit, complete, memorized in your hands. You could seal this. Push it back. Close the lock that's been broken for fifty years.

And you have Mara's pages. Her map. Her symbol taxonomy. Her timeline. She documented everything on the other side. She went through and she's been there, working, mapping, understanding. The Figure across the portal is her invitation.

The portal exhales. The pause. A second and a half. You could act in that window. You could seal it. Or you could step through it.

The portal doesn't care which one you choose. It breathes either way.

{priority == "find_mara":

    * [Seal it.]
        You know how to close it. The pattern. The containment circuit. The key your hands have memorized.
        The Figure stands on the other side. If you seal this, you seal her in. Wherever she is. Whatever she's become. The lock closes from this side only.
        -> buried_ending
    * [Cross.]
        You came to find her. The pattern can wait. The containment can hold a little longer{bex_relationship >= 5:, the way Bex has held it, night after night, for decades}. The cracks aren't going anywhere.
        She's been on the other side long enough.
        -> other_side_ending
}

{priority == "investigate_paranormal":

    * [Seal it.]
        You know how to close it. This is what the Order sent you to do. Identify the threat. Contain it. That's the mission. That has always been the mission.
        The evidence on the other side can stay on the other side. Close the door. Bury it.
        -> buried_ending
    * [Cross.]
        The evidence is on the other side. Mara's been documenting it for weeks. Her pages, her recordings, her map: all of them describe something beyond the portal, something the Order has never seen. The investigation doesn't end at the threshold.
        Going through is the investigation.
        -> other_side_ending
}

= night_5_descent_default

// No ritual knowledge. Other Side conditions not met.
// Player has reached the portal but doesn't have the tools to seal it or the knowledge to cross safely.

You stand at the edge.

The portal breathes. Exhale. Inhale. Exhale. The amber light pulses. The Figure stands on the far side, motionless, waiting.

{has_symbol_knowledge || has_portal_mechanics: You understand the symbols. The containment pattern, the feeding glyphs, the opening marks. You can read the grammar carved into the rim. But reading isn't doing. You don't have the complete containment circuit. You don't know the ritual. You can see the lock but you don't have the key.}

{not has_symbol_knowledge && not has_portal_mechanics: The carvings on the rim are dense and layered. You've seen fragments of them in the tunnels, on the walls, scratched into stone at different depths. But you can't read them. The pattern is there and you can't parse it. The mine showed you pieces and you didn't collect enough of them.}

{has_mara_pages && erasure_level >= 8: Mara's pages are in your pocket. Her map, her taxonomy, her timeline. She went through. You could follow her. But you can feel yourself thinning. The edges of your hands are soft. Your name is a shape your mouth makes without certainty. If you step through in this state, there might not be enough of you left on the other side to find her.}

{not has_mara_pages: The portal is open. You could step through. But you don't know what's on the other side. You don't have a map, a guide, an understanding of what the portal leads to or what survives the crossing. Stepping through without that knowledge isn't investigation. It's falling.}

The portal exhales. The heat. The pressure. The pause. The second and a half of silence.

{has_gloria_medallion: You could step forward. The disc is warm against your chest. You can't close the portal. You don't have the ritual. But the disc hums against the portal's rhythm, pushing back. Gloria said the deep tunnels felt quieter when her father wore it.}
{not has_gloria_medallion: You could step forward. Into the nothing. Into the absence. But you don't have what you need to survive it, or to close it, or to do anything except fall.}

The Figure watches. It doesn't move. It doesn't beckon. It just stands on the other side of something you can't cross.

{has_escape_route: Gloria's truck is behind the diner. The keys are under the mat. The road out of Veinrock runs straight through the desert to the highway. You could climb these steps, walk out of the mine, and drive until the hum fades.}

The portal breathes.

You don't have the key. You don't have the path. But you're here. You made it to the bottom. You saw it. The thing underneath the mine underneath the town. The thing that's been hungry since before any of this was built.

* {has_escape_route} [Climb. Take the truck. Drive.]
    You turn. The spiral steps. Up. Away from the amber light, away from the heat, away from the portal that breathes and breathes and breathes. You climb and the hum fades and the air cools and the utility lights return like stars after a storm.
    Through the tunnels. Through the mine. Out. The cold desert air. The parking lot. The road.
    Gloria's truck. Keys under the mat. The engine turns over.
